  "query_preview": "(Tonight through Tuesday)...\nIssued at 1105 PM EST Mon Nov 16 2026\n\nA relatively uncomplicated forecast is in the offing for the next \nweek, with largely dry and quiet conditions, above normal \ntemperatures through the first part of the coming weekend, and only \na brief opportunity for light showers primarily late Saturday into \nSunday.\n\nAn expansive and strong dome of surface high pressure will remain in \ncontrol across the eastern third to half of the CONUS through the \nend of the work week, with upper level ridging also shifting \neastward into the area and displacing the primary track for \ndisturbances well to our north.\n\nSkies will be clear to partly cloudy throughout the week, with a \nslow but steady warming trend of about 3-4 degrees each day through \nFriday, bringing highs back to well above normal for the time of \nyear, and lows above normal but still crisp many nights with nearly \nideal radiational cooling conditions expected several nights. Some \npatchy fog concern may need watching on several nights, but with \nample sun on many days, along with over 90 percent of the state at \nleast abnormally dry and moderate drought ongoing across a third of \nIndiana, widespread fog concerns should be minimal.\n\nChange comes again over the weekend as the upper level ridge finally \nbreaks down and the surface ridge is shuttled eastward, allowing an \nelongated shortwave to pass through the region. Associated with this \nshortwave looks to be a moderate surface low north of the Great \nLakes with a lengthy cold front but a relatively narrow warm \nsector/moist conveyor belt. The displacement of the more significant \nforcing northward along with the relatively narrow moist conveyor \nbelt should limit precip coverage and amounts to scattered light \nshowers, maximized immediately along and ahead of the front, as this \nwill be the primary forcing source in the immediate region, with \nonly weak upright instability expected if any. Frontal timing and \nold school model K-index values suggest thunder is unlikely.\n\nA return to dry but substantially cooler weather is expected for \nearly next week in the wake of the cold front as another large \nsurface high traverses the area, though this one appears more likely \nto move through quicker than the one anchored over the eastern US \nthis week. Temperatures are likely to be 20 or so degrees colder by \nnext Monday than they were to start the weekend.",
